-- Reading through calls to other rules (specs/todo/WHERE-INLINING-SPEC.md §9).
--
-- An exception, and an offence the exception defeats. A textbook draws this as
-- "[conditions] -> not an offence": the exception is SUFFICIENT for the offence
-- not to be made out. `the arrow is broken` asks for both at once, so if the
-- offence really is defeated by the exception it can never be true.
--
-- Before calls were read through, each call was one opaque leaf, and `the arrow
-- is broken` was two unrelated atoms: satisfiable, no finding, on this encoding
-- AND on the broken one below. The tool could not tell them apart.
--
-- `the arrow is broken in the bad encoding` is the control. Its offence forgets
-- the exception, so both can hold at once and there must be NO finding. If it
-- ever reports unsat, the unfolding is inventing contradictions.

DECLARE Facts HAS
    `the act was accidental` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the act was lawful`     IS A BOOLEAN
    `harm was caused`        IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN f IS A Facts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the exception applies` f IF
        f's `the act was accidental`
    AND f's `the act was lawful`

GIVEN f IS A Facts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence is made out` f IF
        f's `harm was caused`
    AND NOT `the exception applies` f

GIVEN f IS A Facts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the arrow is broken` f IF
        `the exception applies` f
    AND `the offence is made out` f

GIVEN f IS A Facts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence, forgetting the exception` f IF
        f's `harm was caused`

GIVEN f IS A Facts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the arrow is broken in the bad encoding` f IF
        `the exception applies` f
    AND `the offence, forgetting the exception` f
